---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import DotGrid from '../components/DotGrid.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
import ShareRow from '../components/ShareRow.astro';
import LevyChart from '../components/LevyChart.astro';
import BarCompare from '../components/BarCompare.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { scores } from '../data/figures';

// Deep links into the Oct. 19, 2026 Farmington School Board video.
const vid = (seconds: number) => `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qcF6xnfGMWc&t=${seconds}s`;

const ballotText = {
  before:
    '“Shall the Town of FARMINGTON limit property tax growth for FARMINGTON SCHOOL DISTRICT (SAU #61) under RSA 32:5-i? If adopted for a two-year period: (1) the local property tax levy may not grow beyond the prior year’s amount, adjusted for inflation and new construction; (2) SAU central office spending may not exceed 6 percent of total school district appropriations; and (3) bonded capital costs are excluded from both limits. ',
  marked:
    'These caps apply only to administrative operations of the SAU central office and do not affect classroom instruction, school-based services, or other municipal expenditures.',
  after: ' These limits may be overridden as provided in RSA 32:5-i. Adoption requires a three-fifths (3/5) majority vote.”',
};
---
<Base>
  <header class="topbar" slot="header">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#top">
        <svg width="26" height="26" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="64" height="64" rx="12" fill="#14325c"/><path d="M14 46V18h22v6H21v5h13v6H21v11z" fill="#fff"/><circle cx="46" cy="40" r="7" fill="#f2b33d"/></svg>
        <span>Farmington &amp; Question 2</span>
      </a>
      <nav class="topnav" aria-label="Sections">
        <a class="hide-xs" href="#quick">1-minute version</a>
        <a class="hide-sm" href="#ballot">The wording</a>
        <a class="hide-sm" href="#math">The math</a>
        <a class="hide-sm" href="#vote">How to vote</a>
        <a href="#sources">Sources</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- ===================== HERO ===================== -->
  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
      <p class="date-pill"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>{site.electionDateShort} · Question 2 is on the back of your ballot</p>
      <h1>It’s called a tax cap. It’s really a cap on <em>Farmington’s schools.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Question 2 would limit how much Farmington can raise in property taxes for its schools each year. That
        limit covers <strong>everything</strong> the school budget pays for, not just administration. Going over it
        would take a 60% vote.
      </p>
      <p class="lede"><strong>We recommend voting No on Question 2.</strong> Here’s why, in plain English, with a source for every fact.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#quick">Read the 1-minute version</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="#ballot">See the exact ballot wording</a>
      </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="glance card" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
        <h2 id="glance-title" class="kicker">Question 2 at a glance</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>2</dt><dd>caps in one yes-or-no question: one on the school tax levy, one on administration</dd></div>
          <div><dt>60%</dt><dd>“yes” needed to adopt the cap, and again every time a budget needs to go over it</dd></div>
          <div><dt>$360K</dt><dd>first-year gap Farmington’s school leaders project, before special ed, fuel and transportation</dd></div>
          <div><dt>5.73%</dt><dd>Farmington’s central-office share, already under the 6% limit. The levy cap is what bites.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="small muted" style="margin:0">
          <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="The law" /> <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board, Oct. 19" />
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== QUICK ===================== -->
  <section id="quick" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The 1-minute version</p>
      <h2>Five things to know before you vote</h2>
      <ol class="steps prose">
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>It limits all school property-tax money, not just administration.</strong> Question 2 has two
            limits. One caps central-office administration at 6% of the budget. The other caps the school district’s
            whole property-tax levy, which helps pay for teachers, buses, heat and special education.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="The law" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>The ballot’s promise about classrooms only fits half of it.</strong> The ballot says “these caps”
            do not affect classroom instruction. One of the bill’s own sponsors said that sentence describes only the
            6% administration cap. He first said he “wasn’t 100 percent sure” and checked with a Senate lawyer before
            confirming.
            <Cite id="keene-innis" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>The limit grows with general inflation, not with school costs.</strong> It allows growth for
            inflation and new construction. It has no adjustment for enrollment, special-education needs, health
            insurance increases, or cuts in state aid.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Farmington’s own estimate: a $360,000 gap in year one.</strong> District leaders estimate the cap
            would allow about $377,500 more for the 2027–28 budget, while known increases in contracts and health
            insurance alone total about $738,300. That’s before special education, fuel and transportation.
            <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board video" />
            <a href="#farmington">See the Farmington numbers</a>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Once it’s in place, a minority can block a budget.</strong> Any budget over the cap needs 60%
            “yes” to pass. So 41 voters out of 100 could block a budget that 59 of them support.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X" />
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== BALLOT ===================== -->
  <section id="ballot">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Read it closely</p>
      <h2>What the ballot says vs. what the law does</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          Here is Question 2 exactly as it appears on the back of Farmington’s ballot. Look at the highlighted sentence.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <div class="ballot" role="img" aria-label="Replica of Question 2 on the Farmington ballot, with the sentence about classroom instruction highlighted">
            <div class="q">Statutory question required by HB 1300, Chapter 324, 2026</div>
            <p><strong>2.</strong> {ballotText.before}<mark>{ballotText.marked}</mark>{ballotText.after}</p>
            <div class="choices" aria-hidden="true"><span><span class="oval"></span>Yes</span><span><span class="oval"></span>No</span></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Wording from the <Cite id="farmington-sample-ballot" label="Town of Farmington sample ballot" />. The same wording is set by state law for every town. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, V" /></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="stack">
          <caption>Side by side</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">The ballot says</th><th scope="col">The law itself says</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="The ballot says">“These caps apply only to administrative operations of the SAU central office…”</td>
              <td data-label="The law itself says">
                One of the two caps is on the district’s whole property-tax levy: “the estimated amount of local taxes
                to be raised for the fiscal year attributable to the school district.” That money pays for the entire
                school budget, not just the central office. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII & XV" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="The ballot says">“…and do not affect classroom instruction, school-based services…”</td>
              <td data-label="The law itself says">
                Only the 6% administration cap excludes classroom instruction. The levy cap has no such exclusion. If a
                budget would raise more than the cap allows, the state “shall not approve any tax rate that exceeds the
                certified limit” until the budget comes down or 60% of voters override it. The law doesn’t say which
                parts of the budget would be cut. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, IX & XI" />
                <Cite id="vnews-wording" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="The ballot says">“If adopted for a two-year period”</td>
              <td data-label="The law itself says">
                The rules section of the law never sets a two-year limit. Its only end date is January 1, 2032.
                News outlets have described it as a two-year cap (the 2027–28 and 2028–29 school years). Nobody can
                say for sure until the state clarifies.
                <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XII & XVII" />
                <Cite id="rhnh-explainer" />
                <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="The ballot says">“limit property tax growth”</td>
              <td data-label="The law itself says">
                It limits only the school district’s share. It doesn’t limit town, county or state education taxes, so
                your own tax bill can still go up.
                <Cite id="nhpr-explainer" />
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>So, is it really a “tax cap”?</h3>
          <p>
            Partly. It does cap the total amount the school district can raise in property taxes. But it is
            <strong>not</strong> a cap on your tax bill or your tax rate. It doesn’t lower anyone’s taxes, and it lets
            the school levy grow with inflation and new construction. Even a news outlet friendly to the cap wrote
            that it “would not guarantee a particular limit on an individual homeowner’s bill.”
            <Cite id="nhjournal-poll" />
          </p>
          <p>
            Supporters call it the “Taxpayer Protection Act.” <Cite id="housegop-signing" /> A clearer name would be a
            <strong>school funding cap</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The other side, in fairness</h3>
          <p>
            Rep. Ross Berry (R-Weare), who wrote the cap language, says the classroom sentence is accurate. His view is
            that the 6% limit is “the only spending cap that exists,” and the other limit caps taxes, not spending.
            <Cite id="vnews-wording" /> <Cite id="nhb-origin" label="Who wrote it" />
          </p>
          <p>
            Berry also says the two caps belong together: “If you want to cap administrative spending, you have to also
            cap the revenue side of it, because otherwise, they’ll bloat the rest of the budget to increase the 6%.” <Cite id="monitor-confusion" />
          </p>
          <p>
            Two voters sued, saying the wording is misleading. <Cite id="bates-complaint" /> <Cite id="nhb-lawsuit" />
            The courts refused to block the question before the election: Superior Court on Sept. 16, and the NH
            Supreme Court on Sept. 17 and, unanimously, on Sept. 28. No court has ruled on whether the wording itself
            is lawful.
            <Cite id="superior-order" /> <Cite id="nhsc-order-0917" /> <Cite id="nhpr-court" /> <Cite id="indepthnh-supreme" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== MATH ===================== -->
  <section id="math" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The math</p>
      <h2>A limit that grows slower than school costs</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">The formula in the law is short:</p>
        <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem">
          <p style="font:700 1.15rem/1.5 var(--serif);margin:0">
            Next year’s limit = last year’s school tax levy × (1 + inflation + new construction)
          </p>
          <p class="small muted" style="margin:.5rem 0 0">
            Debt payments on approved building projects are left out of the calculation.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII & XVI" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>“Inflation”</strong> means the 12-month change in consumer prices in the Northeast, measured in
            March. Between March 2025 and March 2026 that was about 3.6%.
            <Cite id="bls-cpi-ne" />
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>“New construction”</strong> means new homes, additions and other physical changes. Rising home
            values and town-wide revaluations don’t count.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII(c)" />
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>What’s missing:</strong> the formula has no adjustment for more students, new special-education
            needs, health insurance jumps, or a drop in state or federal aid. New Hampshire’s older tax-cap law, by
            contrast, adjusts for population or enrollment.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-b" />
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            <strong>The state expects it to squeeze budgets.</strong> While lawmakers were weighing the bill, the NH
            Department of Education and Department of Revenue Administration told the Legislature’s budget office that
            where districts adopt the cap, “local school district revenue and school district expenditures could
            decrease.” <Cite id="hb1300-fiscal-note" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">The costs schools can’t control</h3>
      <div class="grid three">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Health insurance</h3>
          <p>
            SchoolCare, which insures nearly 100 New Hampshire school districts and towns, raised rates an average of
            17.4% for July 2026. Plans are written into union contracts, so districts can’t simply switch.
            <Cite id="vnews-health" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Special education</h3>
          <p>
            Federal law requires districts to provide special education “no matter the cost.”
            <Cite id="nhb-sped" /> Local property taxes paid 83% of special-education costs in 2023–24, and in 2024–25
            the state covered only 66% of the aid it owed for the most expensive cases. <Cite id="sped-gap" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>State and federal aid</h3>
          <p>
            The cap limits property taxes, not spending. So if state or federal aid drops, property taxes can’t make up
            the difference without a 60% vote. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XV" /> Rochester’s finance
            director blamed its school budget gap on “the reduction of State and Federal funding.”
            <Cite id="rochester-workshop" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <p>
          <strong>The yardstick can lag.</strong> The cap uses one March inflation number from the year before. In
          March 2021 that number was 2.1%. Over the next 12 months, Northeast prices rose 7.3%.
          <Cite id="bls-cpi-ne" />
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Required costs squeeze everything else.</strong> As the AFT-NH teachers union told lawmakers,
          “Schools are legally required to provide special education services, regardless of cost.” When a capped
          budget has to absorb a required cost, the cuts land on what can legally be cut: staff, electives, sports,
          supplies. <Cite id="aftnh-testimony" />
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
          In fairness: the state’s required teacher pension contribution is scheduled to dip slightly, from 19.23% to
          18.26% of pay, in the first two years a cap would apply. <Cite id="nhrs-rates" />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== FARMINGTON ===================== -->
  <section id="farmington">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What it means for Farmington</p>
      <h2>Farmington isn’t a runaway budget. The cap would still bite.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Farmington’s own school leaders project a $360,000 shortfall in the first year under the cap. That’s in a town
        whose school taxes have grown far more slowly than prices, and whose voters already decide every budget.
      </p>

      <div class="callout bad district-estimate">
        <h3>Farmington’s own estimate: a $360,000 gap in year one</h3>
        <p>
          At the October 19 School Board meeting, district leaders walked through what the cap would mean for the
          2027–28 budget, the first one it would cover. Assuming 3% inflation and little new construction, they estimate
          the cap would allow about <strong>$377,500</strong> more. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2717)} label="Video, 45:17" />
        </p>
        <BarCompare
          label="Farmington School District estimate for 2027-28"
          rows={[
            { label: 'What the cap would allow', value: 377523, display: '$377,523', tone: 'b' },
            { label: 'Known increases: contracts, health insurance (+19%), dental', value: 738287, display: '$738,287', tone: 'c' },
          ]}
        />
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">
          That leaves a gap of about <strong>$360,000</strong>, and it doesn’t yet count special education,
          out-of-district placements, utilities, fuel or transportation.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="Video, 46:12" /> <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2960)} label="49:20" />
          Their conclusion: it’s “pretty safe to say should this tax cap be put into place … that we’ll fall short.”
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3754)} label="1:02:34" />
        </p>
        <p class="small">
          These are the district’s estimates, not final budgets. The gap depends on the inflation figure: if it comes in
          higher than 3%, the cap allows more and the gap shrinks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Already stretched</h3>
        <p>
          District leaders said Farmington has lost about 11 full-time positions over the last couple of years that
          weren’t replaced. The high school has no music teacher of its own, and one librarian covers two schools.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3920)} label="Video, 1:05:20" /> For this school year, the district
          had to add $700,000 to its fallback budget just for rising health insurance costs.
          <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="Deliberative session minutes" />
        </p>
        <p>
          Cuts can’t come from special education, because each student’s plan (IEP) “is a legal document and what is
          promised in that document has to be delivered.” So reductions would fall on regular education.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(4161)} label="Video, 1:09:21" />
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The “administration” half wouldn’t change anything here.</strong> Farmington’s central office already
          costs about 5.73% of the budget, under the 6% limit. The tax levy cap is the part that would bite.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3588)} label="Video, 59:48" />
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid three" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Growing slower than prices</h3>
          <p>
            From 2016 to 2024, Farmington’s day-to-day school spending rose about 10%, while consumer prices rose about
            31%. <Cite id="census-f33-2016" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2024" /> <Cite id="bls-cpi-us" /> Enrollment fell
            by nearly a quarter, so spending per student rose 43%, less than the statewide 50%. Farmington now spends
            about the state average per student.
            <Cite id="census-f33-2016-states" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2024-states" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Voters already decide</h3>
          <p>
            Farmington votes on its school budget by ballot every March. In 2026, voters turned down the proposed
            budget 239–337, so a smaller fallback (“default”) budget took effect.
            <Cite id="farmington-special-warrant-2026" /> <Cite id="farmington-results-2026" /> In 2025, they rejected a petitioned school tax cap, 185–228.
            <Cite id="farmington-results-2025" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Less to draw on</h3>
          <p>
            Farmington’s median household income is $66,943, compared with $99,031 statewide.
            <Cite id="acs-income-farmington" /> <Cite id="acs-income-nh" /> About 4 in 10 Farmington students qualify
            for free or reduced-price lunch, compared with about 1 in 4 statewide.
            <Cite id="nces-lunch-farmington" /> <Cite id="nces-lunch-nh" /> The state puts Farmington in its top school
            building aid tier, reserved for “less affluent communities.” <Cite id="nhed-building-aid" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Even slow growth would hit the cap</h3>
        <p>
          From 2017 to 2025, Farmington’s school tax levy grew 11%, while Northeast prices rose 28%. But the levy goes up
          and down from year to year. Under HB 1300, each year’s limit starts from the year before, so a down year becomes
          the new ceiling. In 4 of those 8 years, the levy rose more than the cap would have allowed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <LevyChart>
        <figcaption>
          Red bars mark years the levy grew faster than the cap’s inflation figure plus the most that new construction
          could have added. Levy: state-approved tax rate sheets in Farmington’s town reports
          <Cite id="town-report-2017" /> <Cite id="town-report-2018" /> <Cite id="town-report-2019" />
          <Cite id="town-report-2020" /> <Cite id="town-report-2021" /> <Cite id="town-report-2022" />
          <Cite id="town-report-2023" /> <Cite id="town-report-2024" /> <Cite id="town-report-2025" label="2025" />.
          Inflation: <Cite id="bls-cpi-ne" />.
        </figcaption>
      </LevyChart>
      <div class="prose">
        <details>
          <summary>How we figured this</summary>
          <p>
            We compared each year’s change in Farmington’s “local education” tax levy with the 12-month change in the
            Northeast consumer price index for March of the year before the school year it paid for, as HB 1300
            specifies. We then added the most that “new construction” could have contributed: the growth in Farmington’s
            total taxable value, which was under 1% in every year without a town-wide revaluation. For the two
            revaluation years (2019 and 2024), we generously assumed 3%; both years were still over. Farmington’s
            school district has no bond payments, so HB 1300’s exclusion for building debt doesn’t change the result.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII" />
          </p>
        </details>

      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Farmington already knows what a tight year looks like</h3>
        <p>
          Farmington’s school finances have had real problems, and it’s fair to say so. The district’s 2023 audit came
          back without an opinion because “inadequate or no documentation exists to support material account balances
          and transactions.” <Cite id="farmington-audit-2023" />
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2025–26 the school district ran short of money. The board chair said problems in the business office, which
          has since been reorganized, were “coupled with unanticipated healthcare bills.” The district froze spending
          and, mid-year, carried out its “first Reduction in Force (RIF) in years.”
          <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="Farmington annual report, pp. 29–30" />
        </p>
        <p>
          A tax cap didn’t cause that shortfall, and a cap won’t fix bookkeeping. But under a cap, rebuilding afterward
          would take a 60% vote for any budget above the limit.
        </p>

        <h3>Students had been gaining ground</h3>
        <p>
          Farmington students have long scored below the state average. Between 2021 and 2024 they closed most of that
          gap in math and reading, and in science they beat the state average in 2022, 2023 and 2025.
          <Cite id="farmington-ar-2025" label="Farmington annual report" /> <Cite id="sdganh-scores" />
        </p>
        <p>
          In spring 2026, the same school year as the freeze and layoffs, scores fell back: reading went from 49% to 41%
          proficient and science from 45% to 26%, while state averages held steady.
          <Cite id="sdganh-scores" /> <Cite id="keene-2026-scores" /> We can’t prove the budget trouble caused the drop.
          Farmington tests only 140 to 430 students a year, and each year tests different kids. But it shows how quickly
          progress can slip.
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>See every year’s scores</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="scores">
              <caption>Percent of students proficient or better (Farmington / state)</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Spring</th><th scope="col" class="num">Math</th><th scope="col" class="num">Reading</th><th scope="col" class="num">Science</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {scores.map((r) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{r.year}</th>
                    {r.year === 2020 ? (
                      <td colspan="3" class="muted">No state tests (COVID-19)</td>
                    ) : (
                      <>
                        <td class="num">{r.math[0]}% / {r.math[1]}%</td>
                        <td class="num">{r.ela[0]}% / {r.ela[1]}%</td>
                        <td class="num">{r.sci[0]}% / {r.sci[1]}%</td>
                      </>
                    )}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small muted">
            All tested grades: math and reading in grades 3–8 plus the grade 11 SAT; science in grades 5, 8 and 11. The
            grade 11 SAT changed format in 2024. 2026 results were released September 2, 2026. Sources: Farmington annual
            reports <Cite id="farmington-ar-2025" label="2025" /> <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="2026" />, state
            test data <Cite id="sdganh-scores" />, and state averages for 2018 <Cite id="nhpr-2018-scores" /> and 2026
            <Cite id="keene-2026-scores" />.
          </p>
        </details>

        <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          Hear it from the people who build the budget: the Farmington School Board’s October 19 presentation on HB 1300
          runs about 35 minutes, with questions from residents.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" label="Watch from 37:21" />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== 60% ===================== -->
  <section id="sixty" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The 60% rule</p>
      <h2>Once it’s in, 41 voters can outvote 59</h2>
      <div class="grid two" style="align-items:center">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            If Question 2 passes in Farmington, any school budget that goes over the limit must win
            <strong>60% “yes”</strong> on the ballot to pass. A majority isn’t enough.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X(a)" />
          </p>
          <p>
            That means a budget supported by 59 out of 100 voters would still fail, and the district would have to stay
            within the cap.
          </p>
          <p>
            HB 1300 also has no way to repeal a cap early. The question comes back on the November 2028 ballot either way.
            <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure style="margin:0">
          <DotGrid yes={59} label="100 dots: 59 voters vote yes on a school budget, 41 vote no. Under the cap, the budget still fails." />
          <div class="legend" style="margin-top:.75rem"><span>59 vote yes</span><span class="b">41 vote no</span></div>
          <figcaption>Under the cap, this budget fails.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== NEARBY ===================== -->
  <section id="nearby">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Caps next door</p>
      <h2>Nearby cities already live with tax caps</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Several cities near Farmington have had tax caps for years. Their own budget records show the same pattern:
        costs rise faster than the cap allows, and the gap is closed by cutting people and programs or by overriding
        the cap.
      </p>
      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Rochester</h3>
          <p>
            In 2026, Rochester cut 22 positions from its school budget. The city’s finance director pointed to an
            “inherent structural deficit in the School Department budget due to the reduction of State and Federal
            funding.” <Cite id="rochester-workshop" />
          </p>
          <p>
            The council used a special exception in the city charter to restore 15 teaching positions.
            <Cite id="rochester-adopted" /> In fairness, Rochester has also often taxed below its cap: $14.5 million
            below what it allowed from 2021 to 2025. <Cite id="rochester-below-cap" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Somersworth</h3>
          <p>
            In 2025, keeping the same school services meant a budget increase of about 6%. Most of that was teacher
            contracts, a 14.4% jump in health insurance, and required special education. Staying within the cap meant
            falling about $1.3 million short, “leading to position cuts and possibly a Career Technical Center program
            cut.” <Cite id="somersworth-workshop" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Franklin</h3>
          <p>
            Franklin’s cap-limited budget for 2026–27 would have eliminated more than 16 positions, including an art
            teacher, a music teacher and a computer teacher. The district faced $500,000 more for health insurance,
            nearly $1 million more for special education, and close to $500,000 less in state aid.
            <Cite id="monitor-franklin" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Dover</h3>
          <p>
            Dover’s council voted to go over its cap in 9 of the 18 budget years from 2009 to 2026, much of it for
            schools. <Cite id="dover-overrides" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout prose">
        <p>
          <strong>One big difference:</strong> in these cities, the elected city council can vote to go over the cap.
          Under HB 1300, going over Farmington’s cap would take 60% of voters at the ballot box.
          <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X(a)" />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== OTHER SIDE ===================== -->
  <section id="other-side" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The other side</p>
      <h2>What supporters say, and what to weigh</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        People backing Question 2 have real concerns about the cost of living. Here are their strongest arguments,
        stated fairly, and the facts we think belong next to them.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="stack">
          <caption>Arguments for the cap</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Supporters say</th><th scope="col">Worth weighing</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>Property taxes are climbing too fast.</strong> Statewide, local property taxes rose 47.5% from
                2016 to 2025, or about 12% after inflation. <Cite id="nhfpi-proptax" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                True, and it hurts. But a big reason is that New Hampshire’s state government pays a smaller share of
                school costs than any other state, leaving more for local property taxes. Question 2 doesn’t change
                that. It only limits the local share. <Cite id="nhb-state-share" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>Enrollment is falling while spending rises.</strong> New Hampshire public schools had 160,323
                students in fall 2025, down from more than 205,000 twenty years earlier. <Cite id="keene-enrollment" />
                A free-market think tank found per-pupil spending nearly doubled after inflation from 2001 to 2024.
                <Cite id="jbc-spending" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                Buses, buildings and heat don’t shrink when a few students leave each grade. <Cite id="keene-enrollment" />
                And statewide trends aren’t Farmington’s story: Farmington’s own spending has grown more slowly than
                inflation (see above).
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>A November vote lets more people decide.</strong> Spring school-cap votes in 2025 drew only about
                20% as many voters as the 2024 presidential election. <Cite id="monitor-caps-2025" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                That’s a fair point. But once a cap is in place, every vote to go over it happens at the regular
                school budget vote, not in November. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X(a)" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>It puts money in classrooms, not administration.</strong> Americans for Prosperity-NH says the
                law “ensures education dollars are spent in the classroom, not on administrative overhead.”
                <Cite id="keene-innis" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                Farmington’s central office already runs under 6%, so that part changes nothing here.
                <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3749)} label="School Board video" /> And research on tax caps in
                other states found the cuts fell on instruction at least as much as on administration.
                <Cite id="figlio-1997" /> <Cite id="figlio-1998" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>Massachusetts has had a tougher cap since 1980.</strong> Supporters note HB 1300’s cap is
                tied to inflation, so it never allows less growth than inflation. <Cite id="jbc-massway" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                Massachusetts’ state government pays about 44% of school funding, compared with 28% in New Hampshire.
                <Cite id="newamerica-ma" /> <Cite id="newamerica-nh" /> And Massachusetts towns can override their
                cap with a simple majority. HB 1300 requires 60%. <Cite id="ma-primer" />
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What research on tax caps has found</h3>
          <p>
            Studies of tax limits in other states mostly found larger classes, lower teacher pay and lower test
            scores, and no drop in administrative costs. <Cite id="figlio-1997" /> Some studies found little effect on
            student performance. <Cite id="ddm-1998" /> And caps do slow tax growth: New York’s cut average school tax
            increases from 6.6% a year to 1.9%. <Cite id="rockinst-ny" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Not just a party-line issue</h3>
          <p>
            In January 2026, the NH House killed a different school budget cap bill 346–9, after Republican Rep.
            Kristine Perez said a study in her town had convinced her that a tax cap is not an ideal way to restrain
            spending. <Cite id="nhb-hb675" /> Voters have also rejected local caps: in March 2026, nine towns voted
            them down. <Cite id="nhpr-rejected" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== UNKNOWNS ===================== -->
  <section id="unknowns">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Honest uncertainty</p>
      <h2>What nobody knows yet</h2>
      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>How long it lasts</h3>
          <p>
            The ballot says “two-year period.” The law’s rules never set a two-year end; the only end date is
            January 1, 2032.
            <Cite id="rhnh-explainer" /> <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XVII" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>How skipped ballots count</h3>
          <p>
            If you leave Question 2 blank, it may not count at all, or it may count against the 60% needed to pass.
            The state Attorney General’s office has not issued guidance. Mark the choice you mean.
            <Cite id="nhpr-cuts" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Which inflation number applies</h3>
          <p>
            The law uses the March figure “in the prior fiscal year,” and the state tax agency hasn’t published rules
            explaining which March. That number sets how much the budget can grow.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII(b)" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What gets cut</h3>
          <p>
            The law doesn’t say which parts of a budget must shrink to fit under the cap. That would be left to
            Farmington’s school board and voters each year.
            <Cite id="vnews-wording" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== VOTE ===================== -->
  <section id="vote" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">How to vote</p>
      <h2>Flip your ballot over</h2>
      <ol class="steps prose">
        <li><p><strong>Vote on {site.electionDate}.</strong> Question 2 is on the general election ballot in every New Hampshire town. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, I" /></p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Turn your ballot over.</strong> Question 2 is on the back. The front says “VOTE BOTH SIDES.” <Cite id="farmington-sample-ballot" /></p></li>
        <li><p><strong>To keep the cap off Farmington’s schools, fill in “No.”</strong> The cap only takes effect if 60% of voters say yes. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VI" /></p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Questions about where or how to vote?</strong> Contact the Farmington Town Clerk. <Cite id="farmington-clerk" /></p></li>
      </ol>
      <div class="callout prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Most people haven’t heard about this yet.</strong> In a September UNH poll, 46% of likely voters
          statewide said they’d vote yes, 33% no, and 21% weren’t sure. Only 8% of Granite Staters said they had heard
          a lot about it. If this
          page helped you, share it with a neighbor. <Cite id="unh-poll" />
        </p>
      </div>
      <ShareRow />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== FAQ ===================== -->
  <section id="faq">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Common questions</p>
      <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <details>
          <summary>Will Question 2 lower my property taxes?</summary>
          <p>
            No. It limits how fast the school district’s total property-tax collection can grow. It doesn’t lower what
            you pay now, and it doesn’t limit town, county or state education taxes.
            <Cite id="nhjournal-poll" /> <Cite id="nhpr-explainer" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>When would the cap start?</summary>
          <p>
            With the budget for the 2027–28 school year, the one Farmington votes on next spring.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XII" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Who decides for Farmington?</summary>
          <p>
            Farmington voters. Farmington’s ballot asks about the Farmington School District (SAU #61). The cap takes
            effect if 60% of voters voting in the district approve it.
            <Cite id="farmington-sample-ballot" /> <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VI" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if Farmington’s schools need more than the cap allows?</summary>
          <p>
            Voters would have to approve going over the cap by 60%. If that vote fails, the state won’t approve a
            school tax rate above the cap, so the budget has to shrink to fit.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X & XI" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>If it passes and doesn’t work, can we undo it?</summary>
          <p>
            Not right away. HB 1300 has no option to repeal a cap early. The question comes back in November 2028, and
            the law itself ends January 1, 2032.
            <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" /> <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XVII" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Would the 6% administration cap save Farmington money?</summary>
          <p>
            Probably not. District leaders say Farmington’s central office costs about 5.73% of the budget, already under
            the 6% limit. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3749)} label="School Board video, 1:02:29" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Couldn’t towns already adopt tax caps?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes. Since 2011, voters have been able to petition for a local cap, which also needs 60% to pass. In
            2025 and 2026 many districts voted on caps and most rejected them. HB 1300 puts the question on every
            ballot automatically. <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How did this end up on the ballot?</summary>
          <p>
            HB 1300 began as a bill about congressional districts. In January 2026, an amendment replaced it with the
            tax cap question. <Cite id="nhb-origin" /> <Cite id="hb1300-text" label="Read the final law" /> The final version passed the House 183–170 and the Senate 15–8,
            and Gov. Kelly Ayotte signed it on July 15, 2026. <Cite id="hb1300-status" />
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:2.5rem">Plain-English glossary</h3>
      <dl class="glossary prose">
        <dt>Levy</dt>
        <dd>The total amount of money the school district raises from property taxes in a year.</dd>
        <dt>Appropriation</dt>
        <dd>The amount voters approve the district to spend.</dd>
        <dt>SAU</dt>
        <dd>School Administrative Unit: the superintendent’s central office. Farmington’s is SAU 61.</dd>
        <dt>Override</dt>
        <dd>A vote to go over the cap. Under HB 1300 it needs 60% “yes.”</dd>
        <dt>Inflation (CPI)</dt>
        <dd>How much everyday prices went up. HB 1300 uses the Northeast Consumer Price Index, measured in March.</dd>
        <dt>New construction</dt>
        <dd>Value added by new buildings and additions. Rising prices of existing homes don’t count.</dd>
        <dt>Bonded capital costs</dt>
        <dd>Loan payments for school buildings. These are left out of the cap.</dd>
        <dt>DRA</dt>
        <dd>The NH Department of Revenue Administration, which approves tax rates and would enforce the cap.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

